  "summary": "48-page hardbacks where every panel is a painting: the album as objet.",
  "description": "Bande dessinée — the Franco-Belgian comic — built a distinct visual culture around the album format: 48-64 page hardbacks in rigid page grids, full-process color from the 1940s, and craft traditions from Hergé's ligne claire to André Franquin's elastic comedy to Moebius's sky-hatching and Bilal's grim airbrush. The album market treats comics as literary objects — critics review them, bookshops shelve them, festivals (Angoulême) award them — and its panel discipline (clean gutters, standardized page counts, painterly backgrounds) gave the world a vocabulary distinct from US action comics and Japanese tankobon. BD style still signals \"European comics\" at a glance: planche over splash, tableaux over speed.",
  "culturalContext": "Bande dessinée made comics literature: the 48-page album, Angoulême's festival prestige and France's bande-dessinée sections in bookshops treat the form as national heritage; BD's panel discipline and painterly craft remain Europe's comic identity against US and Japanese formats.",
